1. Creating and Renaming Files/Directories
•	mkdir test_dir
 
•	touch test_dir/example.txt
 
•	mv test_dir/example.txt test_dir/renamed_example.txt

2. Viewing File Contents
•	Display the complete /etc/passwd file.
       cat /etc/passwd
               cat reads and displays the complete contents of the specified file.
 
•	Display only the first 5 lines.
       head -n 5 /etc/passwd
               head -n 5 displays the first five lines.
 
•	Display only the last 5 lines.
       tail -n 5 /etc/passwd
                tail -n 5 displays the last five lines.
 
3. Searching for Patterns
•	Find all lines containing the word “root”.
grep 'root' /etc/passwd
grep searches each line and prints lines containing the specified pattern.
 
4. Zipping and Unzipping
•	Compress test_dir into test_dir.zip.
zip -r test_dir.zip test_dir
 
•	Extract test_dir.zip into unzipped_dir.
mkdir -p unzipped_dir
unzip test_dir.zip -d unzipped_dir
zip -r recursively archives the directory. unzip extracts the archive into the destination directory.
 
5. Downloading Files
•	Download content from example.com using wget.
wget https://unsplash.com/photos/keyboard-and-stool-in-a-sunlit-room-og8C5C6H-BI
wget retrieves content from a URL.
 
6. Changing Permissions
•	Create secure.txt and make it read-only for everyone.
touch secure.txt
chmod 444 secure.txt
stat -c '%A %a %n' secure.txt
chmod 444 gives read permission to the owner, group, and others, removing write and execute permissions. The resulting permission string is -r--r--r--.
 
7. Working with Environment Variables
•	Set MY_VAR to “Hello, Linux!” using export.
export MY_VAR='Hello, Linux!'
echo "$MY_VAR"
export sets an environment variable for the current shell and child processes. echo verifies its value.

